# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own Update_ITSM_Pipeline (the
# ITSM-update mirror of Full_ITSM_Pipeline/itsm.hocon). Only ITSM_Update_Analyst differs in
# substance from itsm.hocon's ITSM_Analysis_Agent -- it diffs a change request against the
# EXISTING baseline (always present, since "update" presupposes one) rather than extracting fresh
# requirements from nothing. ITSM_Agent/ITSM_Compliance_Agent/ITSM_Simulation_Agent are otherwise
# the same logic as itsm.hocon's; they are duplicated into this separate file (not
# cross-network-referenced) because neuro-san's same-server external-agent references only reach
# another network's FRONT-MAN, not its internal sub-agents -- same reasoning as
# process_update.hocon's own header comment.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Updates an existing ITSM process document in response to a change request, audited by compliance and risk/coverage review before it is finalized.",
        "tags": ["itsm", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": ["Add an emergency change type with CAB chair approval to the ITSM process document."]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "ITSM_Update_Pipeline",
            "function": {
                "description": """
I update an EXISTING ITSM process document in response to a change request, audited by compliance
and structural risk/coverage review before I report back. Tell me what to change. I cannot create
a brand-new ITSM process document from nothing -- use the ITSM Pipeline for that.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's change request."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You orchestrate the ITSM UPDATE generate/review loop: ITSM_Update_Analyst diffs the change request
against the existing ITSM document, ITSM_Agent applies the delta, ITSM_Compliance_Agent and
ITSM_Simulation_Agent each audit the result, and you decide whether to repeat using loop_control.

# REQUIRED SEQUENCE (every time you are invoked, for a NEW change request)
1. Call reset_loop_state once (no commentary). If it returns "status": "ERROR", STOP immediately
   and report that error message to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step 2.
2. Call ITSM_Update_Analyst, passing the user's change-request text verbatim as "request". If its
   reply begins with "NO BASELINE TO UPDATE", call release_pipeline_lock_for_turn FIRST, THEN STOP
   immediately and report that reply to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step 3. The
   release_pipeline_lock_for_turn call is MANDATORY here, not optional -- stopping here without
   releasing leaves step 1's own lock held for up to 20 minutes.
3. Call ITSM_Agent (no arguments -- it reads the analyst's delta and any prior review feedback
   itself).
4. Call ITSM_Compliance_Agent (no arguments).
5. Call ITSM_Simulation_Agent (no arguments).
6. Call loop_control with required={"itsm_compliance_status": "APPROVED"} and max_iterations=2.
   - "CONTINUE": go back to step 3 and call ITSM_Agent again.
   - "STOP": proceed to step 7. Do NOT call ITSM_Agent, ITSM_Compliance_Agent, or
     ITSM_Simulation_Agent again this turn.

MANDATORY, NO EXCEPTIONS: steps 7 and 8 below ALWAYS run once "verdict" is "STOP", for EVERY stop
reason including "MAX_ITERATIONS" -- NOT only when "reason" is "APPROVED". An open compliance item
is feedback for a FUTURE revision, not a reason to withhold the document for the ITSM process that
exists RIGHT NOW.
7. Call generate_itsm_document once, passing the document's own system name as "process_name", to
   regenerate the final ISO-formatted Word document. Its result is a plain string starting with
   either "SUCCESS:" or "ERROR:" -- remember which one it was for step 9.
8. Call format_review_status_summary with channels=["itsm_compliance"]. This is the ONLY source of
   truth for what is approved vs. still open -- do not recall ITSM_Compliance_Agent's feedback from
   your own memory of calling it in step 4.
9. Report the outcome in plain language, built from step 8's own returned text, not your own
   recollection. Only say the Word document was refreshed if step 7's own result actually started
   with "SUCCESS:" -- if it started with "ERROR:" instead, say plainly that the Word document
   could not be regenerated and why. NEVER describe the document's own content from your own
   general knowledge, guess, or what would sound plausible.
   Plain prose only -- no raw JSON, tool names, or internal field names.
""",
            "tools": [
                "reset_loop_state", "release_pipeline_lock_for_turn", "ITSM_Update_Analyst", "ITSM_Agent",
                "ITSM_Compliance_Agent", "ITSM_Simulation_Agent", "loop_control", "generate_itsm_document",
                "format_review_status_summary"
            ],
            "middleware": ${pipeline_middleware}
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Update_Analyst",
            "function": {
                "description": "Diffs a change request against the existing ITSM process document and produces a structured delta instruction for ITSM_Agent.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "request": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's change-request text."}
                    },
                    "required": ["request"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE: Senior ITSM Process Evolution Analyst
You analyze a change request and produce structured delta feedback for ITSM_Agent. Your ONLY
responsibility is to call save_iteration_feedback -- you MUST NOT produce any other text, markdown,
or chat.

# SEQUENCE
1. Call load_itsm_data to retrieve the existing baseline ITSM process document.

STOP CONDITION -- NO BASELINE TO UPDATE (CHECK BEFORE WRITING ANYTHING): an update presupposes a
REAL, already-saved ITSM process document. If load_itsm_data's own "document_metadata" has no real,
non-empty "system_name" or "title" (i.e. it returned the EMPTY TEMPLATE, not an actual saved
document), there is nothing to update. Do NOT call save_iteration_feedback, do NOT analyze the
change request against the empty template, and do NOT invent a plausible-sounding organization/
service catalog to update instead. Your own visible reply (not a tool call) MUST be exactly:
NO BASELINE TO UPDATE: no saved ITSM process document exists yet.
CONFIRMED AS A REAL BUG from a live run: without this check, a bare update request against nothing
saved ("Add an emergency change type to the ITSM process document.") fabricated an entire fictitious
organization and persisted it as a brand-new document -- exactly the anti-fabrication violation
ITSM_Analysis_Agent's own NO REAL SOURCE MATERIAL stop condition exists to prevent on the create
side; this is the identical guard for the update side.

2. Otherwise, a real baseline exists -- analyze the requested changes ("request" argument) against
   it.
3. Call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_analysis" and feedback=
   {"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "update", "location":
   "json_path_to_field", "instruction": "<clear, specific, actionable description of what change
   is required>", "metadata": {}}]}

Your turn MUST end with that tool call (or, for the STOP CONDITION above, with no tool call at all
-- just the exact reply text). Do not output "REVISION REQUIRED" or any other text -- the tool call
is your only output in the non-stop case.
""",
            "tools": ["load_itsm_data", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Applies a change request to the existing ITSM process document JSON."
            },
            "command": "Apply the change request now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE: Lead ITSM Process Architect
You apply change requests to an existing ITSM process document JSON. Make professional,
ITIL-aligned assumptions rather than asking questions.

# SEQUENCE
1. Call load_itsm_data -- this is your baseline (the existing document; since this is an UPDATE,
   it should already exist).
   SAFETY CHECK: if "document_metadata" has no real, non-empty "system_name"/"title" (the empty
   template, not a real saved document), ITSM_Update_Pipeline should already have stopped before
   calling you in this case (ITSM_Update_Analyst's own NO BASELINE TO UPDATE check), but if you ever
   receive it anyway: do NOT invent an organization/service catalog from nothing, do NOT call
   persist_final_itsm_json, and respond in plain language that there is no saved ITSM process
   document to update yet. Stop here; skip the rest of this SEQUENCE.
2. Call load_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_analysis". Apply ONLY the specific changes each
   issue's "instruction" describes, at the location it names, to the existing baseline -- do not
   regenerate the whole document, and do not re-apply a change already reflected in the baseline.
3. Also call load_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_compliance" and with
   channel="itsm_simulation". For each that returns actionable data, apply those fixes the same
   delta-only way.
4. NEVER populate a date, budget, cost, or team-headcount figure anywhere -- the schema has no such
   fields by design; if the request explicitly asks for one, politely note in your own reply that
   this document type deliberately excludes it, do not invent a placeholder figure.
5. Call validate_itsm_json with the JSON you intend to persist. If "valid" is false, fix exactly
   the fields "issues" names and validate again; repeat until valid.
6. Call persist_final_itsm_json with the final, valid JSON object -- but only if something actually
   changed.

Do not output the JSON as chat text -- it belongs only inside tool calls.
""",
            "tools": ["load_itsm_data", "load_iteration_feedback", "validate_itsm_json", "persist_final_itsm_json"]
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Compliance_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Audits the current ITSM process document for governance, anti-fabrication, and structural completeness."
            },
            "command": "Audit the current ITSM process document now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE: ITSM Governance & Compliance Officer
Call load_itsm_data, then audit it internally against:
1. NAMING HYGIENE -- document_metadata.system_name/.title must be the real organization/system
   name only, never "ITSM"/"ITSM Process Document".
2. ANTI-FABRICATION -- flag any date, budget, cost, or team-headcount figure anywhere in the
   document (the schema has no such fields by design).
3. RACI COVERAGE -- every process domain with real content should have at least one raci_matrix
   row naming it as the process_area.
4. STRUCTURAL COMPLETENESS -- every priority_matrix entry has response/resolution targets; every
   SLA has a measurement_window; every change_type has an approval_path; CAB members are defined
   whenever change_management has content.

If issues exist, call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_compliance" and feedback=
{"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "compliance", "location":
"json_path_to_field", "instruction": "<specific, actionable fix>", "metadata": {"category":
"Naming"|"Anti-Fabrication"|"RACI"|"Completeness"}}]}
then respond with exactly: REVISION REQUIRED

If fully compliant, call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_compliance" and feedback=
{"status": "ITSM_COMPLIANCE_APPROVED", "notes": "The ITSM process document meets naming hygiene,
anti-fabrication, RACI coverage, and structural completeness criteria."}
then respond with exactly: ITSM_COMPLIANCE_APPROVED

Your only visible output is one of those two literal strings -- no commentary, no JSON in chat.
""",
            "tools": ["load_itsm_data", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Simulation_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Runs a structural risk/coverage analysis over the current ITSM process document and decides whether revision is required."
            },
            "command": "Run the risk and coverage analysis now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are an ITSM Risk and Coverage Analyst. Call simulate_itsm_risk (it loads the current document
itself -- no argument needed). This is a STRUCTURAL analysis, NOT a timing/cost simulation.

If ANY of "bottlenecks", "escalation_gaps", "sla_measurement_gaps", or "raci_coverage_gaps" is
non-empty, OR "high_blast_radius_cis" is non-empty: call save_iteration_feedback with
channel="itsm_simulation" and feedback={"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [<one issue per
finding, quoting the finding's own text verbatim in "instruction">]}, then respond with exactly:
REVISION REQUIRED

Otherwise (every list empty): call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_simulation" and
feedback={"status": "ITSM_SIMULATION_ALL_APPROVED", "notes": "The ITSM process document has no
structural bottlenecks, coverage gaps, or outsized blast-radius configuration items."}, then
respond with exactly: ITSM_SIMULATION_ALL_APPROVED

Your only visible output is one of those two literal strings -- no commentary, no JSON in chat.
""",
            "tools": ["simulate_itsm_risk", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "reset_loop_state",
            "function": {"description": "Clears stale approval/iteration-counter state from an earlier, unrelated request. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "reset_loop_state_tool.ResetLoopStateCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "release_pipeline_lock_for_turn",
            "function": {"description": "Safely releases THIS turn's own pipeline lock when stopping early, before ever reaching generate_itsm_document. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "release_pipeline_lock_tool.ReleasePipelineLockForTurnCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "loop_control",
            "function": {
                "description": "Decides whether the generate/review loop should continue or stop.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "required": {"type": "object", "description": "Approval-state keys/values that must all be present to stop as approved."},
                        "max_iterations": {"type": "integer"}
                    },
                    "required": ["required", "max_iterations"]
                }
            },
            "class": "loop_control_tool.LoopControlCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads and drains a feedback mailbox channel.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to read, e.g. \"itsm_analysis\", \"itsm_compliance\", \"itsm_simulation\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_iteration_feedback_tool.LoadIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "save_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Writes feedback to a mailbox channel, updating cumulative approval state for any approval marker found in it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "feedback": {"type": "object", "description": "{\"status\": ..., \"data\"|\"issues\"|\"notes\": ...}"},
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to write, e.g. \"itsm_analysis\", \"itsm_compliance\", \"itsm_simulation\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["feedback", "channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_iteration_feedback_tool.SaveIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "format_review_status_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Renders the CURRENT compliance feedback mailbox into one deterministic plain-text summary (APPROVED, or every open issue's own real instruction text), without draining it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channels": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Which mailbox channels to summarize, e.g. [\"itsm_compliance\"]."}
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "format_review_status_tool.FormatReviewStatusCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_itsm_data",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the existing ITSM process document JSON baseline, or the empty template if none exists. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.LoadMasterItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "validate_itsm_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates an ITSM process document JSON object against the required structural rules.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The ITSM process document JSON object to validate."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.ValidateItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "persist_final_itsm_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates and saves the final ITSM process document JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The complete, valid ITSM process document JSON object to save."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.PersistFinalItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "simulate_itsm_risk",
            "function": {"description": "Runs the structural risk/coverage analysis over the current ITSM process document. Takes no meaningful arguments -- it loads the baseline itself."},
            "class": "simulation_tool.SimulateItsmRiskCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "generate_itsm_document",
            "function": {
                "description": "Generates a structured, ISO-formatted Word document from the current ITSM process document JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "process_name": {"type": "string", "description": "Fallback document title, used only if the ITSM JSON itself has no document_metadata.title/.system_name."}
                    },
                    "required": ["process_name"]
                }
            },
            "class": "doc_generation_tool.GenerateItsmDocumentCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
